faq:
  - q: "What is Microsoft's hiring process?"
    a: "Microsoft's hiring process for most roles: (1) Application and resume screen. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, role fit, compensation alignment. (3) Technical or role-specific screen (1–2 rounds depending on role). (4) Virtual onsite loop — 4–5 rounds with different interviewers covering technical skills, behavioral questions, and cultural fit. (5) Hiring manager debrief and offer decision. Microsoft uses a 'as appropriate' hire standard — not a strict 'raise the bar' requirement like Amazon, which means interviewers can recommend hires even if the candidate is not stronger than all current employees."
  - q: "What does Microsoft look for in candidates?"
    a: "Three core attributes under Microsoft's 'culture add' framework: (1) Growth mindset — the belief that skills are developed through effort and learning, not fixed ability. Microsoft culture was famously rebuilt around this principle by Satya Nadella. (2) Customer focus — understanding who the customer is and what they need, even for internal roles. (3) Diverse and inclusive — a genuine track record of building inclusive teams and environments. Additionally, role-specific technical depth and leadership scope appropriate to the level."
  - q: "How do Microsoft's software engineering interviews work?"
    a: "Microsoft SWE interviews typically include: coding rounds (similar to LeetCode medium–hard), design rounds (system design for senior levels), and behavioral rounds. Microsoft's coding interviews are often more focused on working code and problem-solving process than on optimal complexity — explain your thinking as you code. For senior levels (Senior SWE, Principal), system design and cross-functional leadership are assessed heavily. Microsoft does not use the same structured scoring as Google or Amazon — interviewers have more discretion."
  - q: "Is it easier to get a job at Microsoft than Google or Amazon?"
    a: "Microsoft has a higher acceptance rate than Google for comparable roles — partly because Microsoft hires at a larger scale and partly because the bar is set at 'as appropriate' rather than 'raise the bar.' That said, senior and principal-level roles at Microsoft (L63+) are extremely competitive, particularly in Azure, AI, and core product teams. The hiring bar for entry-level and mid-level roles (L59–L62) is more accessible than equivalent levels at Google or Meta."
  - q: "How important is growth mindset in Microsoft interviews?"
    a: "Very important — Microsoft interviewers are trained to assess it explicitly. The best way to demonstrate growth mindset in an interview: talk about what you learned from failures (not just what you achieved), show curiosity about how things work and why, and demonstrate that you actively seek feedback and incorporate it. Candidates who only describe successes without reflecting on the learning process are often scored lower on culture fit at Microsoft."
